    {
      "id": "d96edeaf93963cf077706ef6fe665ae358c186d0",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 461605468,
          "type": null,
          "body": "I have no opinion either way, but manually releasing memory just before process termination is a bit of a holy war.  See http://c-faq.com/malloc/freeb4exit.html",
          "author": {
            "id": 6621762,
            "username": "graham.bloice",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Graham Bloice",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/8730bfac2267cb97ff1f8187d285f244745a03a783055c8fd6fcd679bfa71c6e?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/graham.bloice"
          },
          "created_at": "2020-12-07T13:49:53.443Z",
          "updated_at": "2020-12-07T13:49:53.443Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 80826945,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 1160,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "efef39df1cd37e226685d4dde2442064a5e94a62",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 461691640,
          "type": null,
          "body": "Thanks for the link. Under the \"further explanation\" link:\n\n> Finally, quite aside from what the operating system is or isn't guaranteed to do, there are questions of what a responsible programmer ought to do. Under some circumstances, it is a very good idea to take the trouble to free all (or most) memory. Under some other circumstances, it is clearly more trouble than it's worth to free memory -- if a program has lots of complicated, interconnected, dynamically-allocated memory structures, and if the program is about to exit, going to great lengths to free all the memory might add nothing more to the program beyond a bigger code-space footprint, a slightly longer running time, and a higher probability of bugs.\n\n...\n\n> if you're using an automated ``leak tracing'' tool, which warns you about any block of memory which you allocate but neglect to free, and which you're using in an attempt to track down a memory leak in a portion of your code which is constantly allocating and freeing memory, you may find it useful to ``fix'' all of the allocations, that is, to explicitly free every scrap of memory you ever allocate, even those one-time allocations which you would have been content to have the operating system implicitly clean up for you, so that the automatic tool's extra complaints abut those one-time allocations won't make it harder to see its complaints about the real leaks which you do care about tracking down and fixing.\n\nI think that since this is simple to fix and potentially useful for cleaning further leak tracing and code analysis tools, it's worth the extra few lines of code to `free()` the memory.",
          "author": {
            "id": 3194989,
            "username": "moshekaplan",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Moshe Kaplan",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://gitlab.com/uploads/-/system/user/avatar/3194989/avatar.png?v=1789950604",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/moshekaplan"
          },
          "created_at": "2020-12-07T15:18:41.564Z",
          "updated_at": "2020-12-07T15:18:41.564Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 80826945,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
